# Known matches for the credential-shape scan, each one deliberate.
#
# A baseline, not a mute. Anything matching a credential shape that is NOT on
# this list fails the build, so adding a real secret is caught while these
# fixtures stay readable. Every line here is a fake value that exists to prove a
# protection works.
internal/transport/git_test.go: asserts Describe() never prints a credential; the value is invented
internal/plant/plant_test.go: asserts the operator instructions carry no credential; these are the shapes it checks for
.github/workflows/validate.yml: the scan's own pattern
SECURITY.md: names the redaction prefixes so a reporter knows what is already masked
docs/plans/2026-09-06-a1-conformance-suite.md: the conformance plan quotes the redaction fixtures
docs/specs/2026-08-31-default-safe-execution-design.md: the design quotes the shapes cap_redact masks
station/bash/caplib.sh: cap_redact's own patterns - the shapes it exists to mask
station/powershell/caplib.psm1: the same patterns in .NET regex - the second implementation of the redactor
station/bash/transports/git.sh: the credential diagnosis names the token-in-URL shapes it tells apart, with invented values
tests/conformance/conformance.sh: the capture contract proves redaction with invented values
tests/fixtures/redaction-corpus.txt: the corpus IS the fake credentials - every MASK line is a shape that must never survive a capture, and the file is nothing else
tests/test-redaction-corpus.sh: the control secret it masks each mutant against, so a broken redactor is told apart from a blind one
tests/test-redact.sh: every fixture is a fake credential that must be seen to be masked
tests/test-start.sh: asserts the preflight masks an invented token
tests/test-transport-contract.sh: asserts tp_describe never returns the invented token in a remote URL
site/content/secrets.md: the secrets page names the shapes cap_redact masks, and shows a before/after with invented values
